      "selection_rationale": "The fresh 2026 guidance change creates an actionable reading question about revenue strength versus earnings exposure, not a recycled hedge-accounting explainer. Concrete reported quarterly gains make a serious countercase to a simple deterioration narrative. Only Southwest selected; prior oil, IBM, Tesla and Meta remain handled.",
      "source_pack": [
        {
          "browser_rendered_acquisition": null,
          "canonical_content_sha256": "711e372d5d722a62a8ba101d5c243b4bad8444d1b6738306e6d8b5f4a4bdb4bc",
          "canonical_content_text": "Southwest Airlines cuts profit forecast as fuel costs overshadow turnaround gains\n\nThe Dallas-based carrier expects adjusted earnings of $2.60 to $2.85 per share in 2026, down from its earlier forecast of $3.25 to $4.25. The midpoint of the new range is below ​analysts' average estimate of $2.85 per share, according to data compiled by LSEG.\n\nSouthwest's average passenger fare jumped nearly 27% in the third quarter, helping lift revenue to a third-quarter ​record of $8.4 billion. Southwest also reported higher adjusted operating margin despite sharply higher fuel costs.\n\nSouthwest's fuel bill rose $821 million from a year earlier, and the carrier expects jet fuel prices to increase further in the final three months of the year.\n\nThe company reported third-quarter adjusted earnings of ​66 cents per share, ​above analysts' expectations of ⁠58 cents and up from 11 cents a year earlier.",
